{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Lee. You’ve been admitted for your shortness of breath and chest pain. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:18:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’m okay right now—no chest pain. But I’ve been really short of breath lately, even walking just a few steps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. That’s concerning. You mentioned this started a while ago with orthopnea—trouble breathing lying flat—and you lost 25 pounds after increasing your furosemide?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:21:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. My breathing at night got better after the diuretic change, but I still can’t walk far without getting winded."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:22:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And the chest pain—can you describe it again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s odd. Sometimes sharp, sometimes dull. It comes and goes, at rest or walking, but doesn’t get worse with exertion. I can still climb stairs without it flaring up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:24:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. We’re worried about heart-related causes, especially given your history—hypertension, high cholesterol, family history of early heart disease."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:25:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had a stress echo already, but they said I didn’t reach target heart rate. And the VQ scan ruled out a clot?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:26:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The echo was limited, and the VQ scan showed low probability for pulmonary embolism. So we’re proceeding with a cardiac catheterization tomorrow to look directly at your heart arteries."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that safe with my kidney issues? I remember last time they held my blood pressure meds before the procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:29:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Very good point. We’ll give you IV fluids and bicarbonate to protect your kidneys. We’ve held your lisinopril and furosemide tonight, and we’ll restart them after the procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:30:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. And my transplant meds—should I take them tonight?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, please take your tacrolimus, mycophenolate, and prednisone as usual. The renal team is following you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:32:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ve also had this dry cough for two weeks—since bronchitis. But it’s getting better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2156-08-22 19:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good to know. The cough started after your last hospitalization, but your breathing exam today is clear—no crackles. So it’s likely not heart failure now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2156-08-23 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs look stable. Your creatinine was 3.0 last night and is now 2.8—improving. Potassium is normal, and your tacrolimus level is on target at 6.6."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2156-08-23 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s reassuring. Is the cath scheduled for today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2156-08-23 08:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, later this morning. We’ll look at your coronary arteries and measure pressures in your heart chambers. If we find a significant blockage, we may place a stent."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2156-08-23 08:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I be awake? Is it painful?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2156-08-23 08:05:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be lightly sedated. You may feel some pressure, but not pain. The team will keep you comfortable throughout."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, we just finished the catheterization. You did well. We found blockages in two arteries. The main one was in a branch of the left circumflex, with 70% narrowing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Did you fix it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we placed a stent to open it up. The other artery—the RCA—is completely blocked, but it’s being bypassed by collateral vessels. We’ll monitor that with symptoms or a future stress test."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no more blockages to fix now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:05:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. The stent is working well. We also measured pressures in your heart—your left side was a bit high, which explains the shortness of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my aortic stenosis? Is that worse?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:08:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Still moderate. The gradient was 37 mmHg, similar to before. We’ll continue to monitor it, but it’s not the main cause of your symptoms now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:09:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What about medications?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:10:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re restarting your furosemide and increasing lisinopril to 10 mg daily to help with blood pressure and heart pressures. And you’ll now start clopidogrel—Plavix—for the stent."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Plavix? I’m already on aspirin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:13:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you’ll take both—dual antiplatelet therapy. It helps prevent clots in the stent. You need to take Plavix every day for at least a month, maybe longer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:14:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Any changes to my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:15:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re adjusting doses—labetalol is now 600 mg total daily in three doses, amlodipine is 10 mg but we’re lowering it to 5 mg twice daily. Your statin is also being reduced to 20 mg rosuvastatin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Why lower the statin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2156-08-23 13:18:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Because of your kidney function and drug interactions. We want to balance heart protection with safety."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re doing well. Your breathing is stable, and labs are unchanged. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take all your meds as prescribed—especially Plavix and aspirin. Watch for bleeding, chest pain, or worsening shortness of breath. Follow up with cardiology and your transplant team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the stent—will I feel a difference?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Many patients feel better within days. Your chest pain should improve, and you may find it easier to walk. But take it slow at first."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I appreciate the care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2156-08-24 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll see you in follow-up. Call if anything changes before then."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2156-08-22 19:17:00",
    "admission_end": "2156-08-24 16:06:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old woman with a history of renal transplant, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and moderate aortic stenosis was admitted for progressive dyspnea on exertion and atypical chest pain. Workup included a dobutamine stress echo, which was limited, and a low-probability VQ scan, ruling out pulmonary embolism. She underwent elective left and right heart catheterization, which revealed two-vessel coronary artery disease with a 70% stenosis in the mid OM2 branch of the circumflex artery. A bare metal stent was successfully placed. Hemodynamics showed moderately elevated left-sided filling pressures and moderate aortic stenosis. Lisinopril was uptitrated to 10 mg daily, and clopidogrel was initiated for dual antiplatelet therapy. Her transplant medications were continued without change. She was discharged home in stable condition with follow-up plans in cardiology and transplant clinics.",
    "problems": [
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Aortic valve disorders",
      "Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, unspecified, with chronic kidney disease stage I through stage IV, or unspecified",
      "Chronic kidney disease, unspecified",
      "Complications of transplanted kidney",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Personal history of transient ischemic attack (TIA), and cerebral infarction without residual deficits",
      "Surgical operation with transplant of whole organ causing abnormal patient reaction, or later complication, without mention of misadventure at time of operation"
    ]
  }
}
